l Ia peaking at +0.9 days must be declining in r by day 5 so flux brighter than ever observed would rule out the model."},"weakest_point":"The Ia weight is only 0.15 and the r-band appears to flatten or fade from 18.87 to 19.05 mag three to one days before the model peak with a six-day gap in coverage leaving the rise poorly constrained."},{"argument":"The light curve shows a ~10 d gradual rise from ~32 uJy to ~90 uJy with red g-r ~0.2 mag and i-band non-detections, consistent with a compact stripped progenitor expanding and cooling toward a Ni-powered peak. The long pre-explosion limits to ~20.5 mag exclude a luminous extended plateau and the Ib/c fit achieves chi2 ~0.54 with no host contamination at b=-30 deg.","hypothesis":"ibc","refuted_if":{"band":"ztfr","day":3,"direction":"above","flux":120,"why":"A stripped-envelope Ib/c should peak and start fading and reddening now, so further brightening well above the current ~87 uJy rules out Ni-decay turnover."},"weakest_point":"The fit requires z=0.005 which makes M ~ -12.6 mag implausibly faint for a normal SN Ib/c and the Type II model carries ~5x higher weight."}],"weights":{"ia":0.3214,"ibc":0.0789,"ii":0.3822,"null":0.2076,"tde":0.0099},"wording":{"term":"about as likely as not","text":"It is about as likely as not that the object will look brighter three days from now than it did at its last brightness check.","words_match":true}},"prev":"a293b9d6a1664c0eda4e6014e41e12bdb5d76bfb62468cb78cbb3d570cac64a8","seq":268,"time":"2026-10-29T01:08:10+00:00","v":2}